==Old Norse==

===Etymology===
From {{proto|Germanic|twai|lang=non}}, whence also Old English {{term|twa|twā|lang=ang}} (English {{term|two}}), Old Frisian {{term|twa|twā|lang=ofs}}, Old Saxon {{term|twa#Old Saxon|twā}}, {{term|two#Old Saxon|twō}}, Dutch {{term|twee}}, German {{term|zwei}}, Gothic {{term|𐍄𐍅𐌰𐌹|tr=twai|sc=Goth}}. Compare also Old English {{term|twegen|twēġen}} (English {{term|twain}}), Old Saxon {{term|twene|twēne}}, Old High German {{term|zwene|zwēne|lang=goh}} (German {{term|zween}}). Ultimately from {{proto|Indo-European|dwó|lang=non}}, whence also Latin {{term|duo|lang=la}}, Ancient Greek {{term|δύο|tr=dúo|lang=grc|sc=polytonic}}.

===Numeral===
{{infl|non|numeral}}

# {{cardinal|lang=non}} [[two]]

